"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,610\nThe Learning Brain. Series 8: Assessment That Teaches\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,365\nEpisode 7\n\n3\n00:00:07,445 --> 00:00:09,716\nBetter multiple choice\n\n4\n00:00:12,516 --> 00:00:15,225\nMultiple choice has a reputation problem.\n\n5\n00:00:15,577 --> 00:00:21,524\nCritics say it only tests recognition: students spot the right answer instead of recalling it.\n\n6\n00:00:22,001 --> 00:00:25,173\nCan a multiple choice question make students really think?\n\n7\n00:00:25,343 --> 00:00:26,844\nIn 2012, Jeri Little,\n\n8\n00:00:27,134 --> 00:00:32,411\nElizabeth Bjork, Robert Bjork and Genna Angello tested this.\n\n9\n00:00:32,875 --> 00:00:39,460\nStudents read passages, then took practice questions in multiple choice or short answer format,\n\n10\n00:00:39,605 --> 00:00:41,115\nbefore a final test.\n\n11\n00:00:41,285 --> 00:00:44,911\nBoth formats helped students remember the tested information.\n\n12\n00:00:45,354 --> 00:00:47,885\nBut multiple choice had an extra benefit.\n\n13\n00:00:48,375 --> 00:00:55,024\nIt also improved recall of related information that appeared only as the wrong answer options.\n\n14\n00:00:55,194 --> 00:00:55,752\nWhy?\n\n15\n00:00:55,860 --> 00:00:57,813\nWhen the wrong options are plausible,\n\n16\n00:00:57,987 --> 00:01:00,978\nstudents have to think through why each one is wrong.\n\n17\n00:01:01,426 --> 00:01:04,630\nThat means retrieving what they know about every option,\n\n18\n00:01:04,832 --> 00:01:06,195\nnot just the right one.\n\n19\n00:01:06,365 --> 00:01:09,350\nThe key condition is competitive alternatives.\n\n20\n00:01:09,713 --> 00:01:12,481\nIf the wrong answers are obviously wrong,\n\n21\n00:01:12,701 --> 00:01:14,254\nthere is nothing to think about,\n\n22\n00:01:14,492 --> 00:01:16,265\nand the benefit disappears.\n\n23\n00:01:16,435 --> 00:01:18,170\nSo the classic weak question,\n\n24\n00:01:18,225 --> 00:01:21,196\nwith one plausible answer and three silly ones,\n\n25\n00:01:21,456 --> 00:01:22,928\nwastes the opportunity.\n\n26\n00:01:23,394 --> 00:01:24,949\nSo does all of the above,\n\n27\n00:01:25,090 --> 00:01:27,661\nwhich can often be spotted without much thought.\n\n28\n00:01:27,831 --> 00:01:32,680\nMultiple choice also makes frequent quizzing practical in large classes,\n\n29\n00:01:32,991 --> 00:01:36,563\nwhich brings in the testing effect from the first two episodes.\n\n30\n00:01:36,733 --> 00:01:37,549\nFor teaching.\n\n31\n00:01:37,936 --> 00:01:41,306\nWrite wrong options from real student misconceptions.\n\n32\n00:01:41,560 --> 00:01:45,478\nMake every option plausible to someone who has not learned the material.\n\n33\n00:01:45,852 --> 00:01:49,639\nAfter the quiz, discuss why each wrong answer is wrong.\n\n34\n00:01:49,991 --> 00:01:54,697\nOne tip to share with students: ask why each wrong answer is wrong.\n\n35\n00:01:54,867 --> 00:01:57,047\nA good wrong answer is a teaching tool.\n\n36\n00:01:57,278 --> 00:01:58,611\nWrite them with care.\n\n"